;; spec:   stdlib §2.5 (about n/32 allocations instead of 2.09 per element), §8.1 item 5 (count cases)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 1024
;; audit:  clean
;; allocs: <= 128
;; roots:  ../../lib support
;; covers: vec
;; A deliberately loose bound (the kind `bound-`): vec of an Array of 1024 elements allocates 78 objects
;; (measured), the bound is n/16 + 64 = 128, and the conj loop that was the default allocated 2143 (measured
;; with the compiler built before the builder). What the case claims is the order of growth, not the constant: case 491 is the same
;; program over ten times as many elements with the bound n/16 + 64 again, and the count there is 678,
;; about 8.7 times this one, where a per-element cost would make it twenty. n/16 and not the page's n/32:
;; each 32 elements are two objects, the leaf's array and the VLeaf node that holds it (the page's
;; bound is case 493, open until a node costs nothing).
(ns main (:use fib.core fib.seq fib.coll))

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((v (vec (array 1024 0))))
    (count v)))
